The Security Council,
Recalling its resolution 2712 (2023) and demanding its full and immediate implementation,
Reaffirming the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations and the inherent right of all States to individual and collective self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the Charter,
Reaffirming that all parties to the conflict must comply with their obligations under international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and recalling the principles of distinction, proportionality and precautions in attack,
Condemning all attacks against civilians and civilian objects, including the heinous terrorist attacks by Hamas that took place in Israel starting on 7 October 2023 and the taking of hostages, and condemning all acts of violence and violations of international humanitarian law, including indiscriminate attacks,
Expressing grave concern at the catastrophic humanitarian situation in the Gaza Strip, including the acute shortages of food, water, fuel, medicine and medical care, the collapse of health services, the outbreak of disease, and the impact on children, women, older persons and persons with disabilities,
Rejecting forced displacement of the civilian population, including children, in violation of international law, and recalling the obligation to ensure the safe return of displaced persons,
Expressing deep concern at attacks on medical and humanitarian personnel, United Nations facilities, including UNRWA shelters, and critical civilian infrastructure, and stressing the need to protect humanitarian relief personnel and ensure the inviolability of United Nations premises,
Noting with grave concern the regional dimensions of the conflict and calling upon all States and actors in the region to exercise maximum restraint and avoid further escalation,
Welcoming the diplomatic efforts by the Secretary-General, regional and international actors to secure the release of hostages, provide humanitarian assistance and promote de-escalation,
Stressing that a just, lasting and comprehensive peace must be based on the two-State solution, with Israel and Palestine living side by side in peace and security within recognized borders, and stressing the importance of the Gaza Strip being reunited with the West Bank under the Palestinian Authority,
1. Demands that all parties fully comply with their obligations under international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and fully implement resolution 2712 (2023), including with regard to the protection of civilians, especially children;
2. Condemns all attacks against civilians and civilian objects, including the terrorist attacks by Hamas, and demands that all parties refrain from acts of violence and violations of international humanitarian law;
3. Demands the immediate and unconditional release of all hostages held by Hamas and other groups, especially children, as well as ensuring immediate humanitarian access to them, and calls for the provision of information on their well-being;
4. Calls for urgent and extended humanitarian pauses and corridors throughout the Gaza Strip for a sufficient number of days, respected by all parties, to enable the full, rapid, safe and unhindered humanitarian access of United Nations humanitarian agencies and their implementing partners, the International Committee of the Red Cross and other impartial humanitarian organizations;
5. Demands that all parties allow, facilitate and enable the immediate delivery of humanitarian assistance to civilians throughout the Gaza Strip, including food, water, fuel, medical supplies, electricity and emergency repairs to essential infrastructure, and ensure the safe movement of aid convoys, medical evacuations and civilians;
6. Demands that all parties protect medical personnel, hospitals, medical facilities, ambulances, United Nations personnel and premises, humanitarian workers and journalists, and refrain from attacks against them, in accordance with international humanitarian law;
7. Rejects forced displacement of the civilian population in the Gaza Strip, demands that all parties refrain from such acts, and calls for the safe return of displaced persons;
8. Calls for urgent steps to create conditions for a sustainable cessation of hostilities, including through the implementation of humanitarian pauses and the release of hostages, and calls upon all parties to engage constructively in diplomatic efforts to this end;
9. Calls on all parties to refrain from actions that could lead to regional escalation, and calls for respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of all States in the region, including Lebanon;
10. Reaffirms its unwavering commitment to the two-State solution and stresses the importance of unifying the Gaza Strip with the West Bank under the Palestinian Authority, and calls for renewed efforts to achieve a just, lasting and comprehensive peace;
11. Requests the Secretary-General to report orally to the Security Council at its next mandated meeting on the implementation of this resolution, and further requests the Secretary-General to identify options to effectively monitor the implementation of this resolution as a matter of prime concern;
12. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

